course: dictionary
lesson: 11248
title: A worrier
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A worrier is a person who worries a lot, often about small things or things that may never happen. The word comes from worry, and the -er ending means a person who does something, like a runner runs.
explain: Look at the context: «Oh my ears and whiskers, how late it's getting!» The speaker keeps fretting about the time, and the exclamation marks show how anxious he feels. So a worrier is someone whose mind jumps straight to what could go wrong.
explain: Worry is normal, and everyone feels it sometimes. If worry gets big, it helps to tell someone you trust, such as a parent, a friend or a teacher.
fact: A worrier is a person who worries a lot.
fact: Worrier is a noun, and its plural is worriers.
fact: Useful phrases: a born worrier, a bit of a worrier, a natural worrier, a big worrier.
fact: «Oh my ears and whiskers, how late it's getting!» shows a funny, exaggerated worrier.
fact: Worry is normal, and telling someone you trust can help.
exercise: What is a worrier? | A person who never sleeps | A person who runs very fast | *A person who worries a lot, often about small things | A person who laughs at every joke
exercise: «Oh my ears and whiskers, how late it's getting!» What does this line show about the speaker? | *A funny, exaggerated worrier who fusses about being late | A calm person who never checks the time | A person who is very good at maths | A person who is angry with the clock
exercise: Which phrase means someone who worries a lot by nature? | A quick snack | A loud shout | A bright morning | *A born worrier
test: Mia says, «I'm a bit of a worrier.» What does she mean? | She is a champion runner | She is very sleepy | *She often worries, perhaps about small things | She never feels any worry at all
test: Why is it good to tell someone you trust when worry gets big? | *It helps you feel less alone with your worry | It makes the worry grow bigger | Worry only lasts a day, so nothing helps | You should hide it from everyone
exam: Which sentence uses worrier correctly? | A worrier is a very large bird that lives in the sea | Worriers is a colour of paint | The worrier at the party laughed loudly and never checked the time | *Ella is such a worrier that she checks the front door three times before bed
